<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">4.1 to 4.5</p>
    <h2 id="t-gravity-is-the-geometry">Gravity as the Geometry Itself</h2>
    <p class="lede">General relativity already says that gravity is curvature rather than a force.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The framework takes that at its word and asks what follows if the
      curvature in question is the same closure that let anything exist at all.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G_{\mu\nu}+\Lambda g_{\mu\nu}=\frac{8\pi G}{c^4}T_{\mu\nu}
    \]</p>
    <p>TTI interprets this geometric description as a clue, not a solution to quantum gravity. It assigns
      gravity to a proposed <strong>π-level</strong> of closure and quantum fields to a later
      <strong>i-level</strong> of complex phase. That vocabulary may organise the framework, but it does not
      explain why a quantum theory of spacetime is unnecessary. Black-hole interiors and the earliest cosmic
      conditions are precisely regimes in which established theories indicate that both quantum effects and
      dynamical geometry matter. Any claim that gravity is the stage rather than an actor must reproduce those
      regimes mathematically. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Three images follow, and each must remain visibly an image. Relativistic orbital precession produces
      a rosette-like path rather than a closed Newtonian ellipse; calling it a spiral highlights cumulative
      advance but does not alter the relativistic mechanism. Gravitational waves are propagating distortions
      of spacetime measured through strain; &ldquo;π vibrating&rdquo; is TTI&rsquo;s metaphor for them, not their
      field-theoretic definition. Finally, curvature can encode the continuing influence of a source, but the
      phrase &ldquo;gravity is memory&rdquo; becomes physics only if a memory variable predicts something beyond
      Einstein&rsquo;s equations. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

