<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Dark energy &mdash; the baseline curvature <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>In the base ΛCDM model, accelerated expansion is represented by a cosmological constant with
        approximately uniform energy density. Other live possibilities include evolving fields and modified
        gravity. TTI selects a geometric interpretation: the baseline from which further integration begins.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>The framework then asks whether separating its proposed π-level geometry from i-level quantum fields
        could reframe the <strong>cosmological constant problem</strong>. Estimates of the mismatch between
        naive vacuum-energy calculations and the observed scale vary with assumptions and can become enormous.
        Calling the levels different does not yet solve that problem: TTI must show, in a calculation, why the
        quantum contributions do not gravitate in the usual way while preserving the successful predictions of
        quantum field theory and general relativity. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Dark matter &mdash; partial integration <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></h3>
      <p>The chain does not require every integration to reach the end. Something may reach the π-level
        &mdash; producing gravity, curving spacetime, carrying mass-energy &mdash; without reaching the
        i-level, and so never interact electromagnetically.</p>
      <p>The proposal is motivated by several observed features: an unseen component clusters, contributes
        to lensing and cosmic structure, and has not yet been identified in a non-gravitational detector.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> But matching that verbal profile is only a first filter. To claim that
        partial integration <em>must</em> exist, TTI still has to derive the same distribution across galaxies,
        clusters, the microwave background and structure growth with one dynamical model.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame frame--creed">
      <p class="lede">Dark matter built the scaffolding. Baryonic matter climbed it. We are the
        climbers.</p>
      <p class="muted">Without the twenty-seven percent there would be no five percent: the early density
        variations needed that gravitational pull to grow fast enough, and the halos are the scaffolding
        inside which ordinary matter collected, cooled and formed stars. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
        The partial integration built the structure that made complete integration possible.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

