<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the dark sector, said in one breath</p>
    <h2 id="t-dark-sector">The Dark Sector Is a Statement About Completion</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The three parts of the budget are not three substances. On this framework's
      reading they are one process caught at three stages, and the only thing that distinguishes them is
      how far along the chain α &rarr; π &rarr; π/2 &rarr; i a region managed to get.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p>Dark energy is geometry that never left the baseline: it expands, and it neither clumps nor shines.
        Dark matter is geometry that reached the π/2 threshold and halted: it has the curvature, so it
        gravitates, but the complex phase never completed, so it cannot couple to light. Baryonic matter is
        geometry that finished: curvature <em>and</em> phase, which is to say gravity and electromagnetism
        and therefore chemistry, bodies and observers. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="lede">Dark matter is dark for a structural reason, not an accidental one. It is not a
        particle that happens to interact weakly. It is a region that stopped one link short of being able
        to interact at all.</p>
      <p>A provisional χ profile can reproduce the scaling of a flat rotation curve, but it does not do so
        &ldquo;for free&rdquo;. If χ &prop; 1/r is assumed, then ρ &prop; 1/r², M(r) &prop; r and the
        orbital speed approaches a constant. The arithmetic is straightforward; the physical burden is not.
        The 1/r profile must be derived from the framework's field equations and then tested against lensing,
        clusters and non-flat features in real rotation curves. The full calculation and its derivation gap are
        stated in <a href="/cosmic-ledger/#t-flat-curves">the Cosmic Ledger</a>.</p>
      <p class="muted">And the limit of the claim, stated here so that it is not mistaken later: this
        account says nothing whatever about <em>which way</em> a galaxy turns. Completion and orientation
        are different axes. Spin belongs to <a href="/moebius/#t-handedness">the seam</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A reader&rsquo;s map of the established physics</p>
    <h2 id="t-gravity-primer">What Gravity and the Dark Sector Mean Before TTI Interprets Them</h2>
    <p>In general relativity, gravity is not treated as an invisible pull laid on top of space. Matter and
      energy affect spacetime geometry, and that geometry determines which paths freely falling objects take.
      The compact statement is Einstein&rsquo;s equation, \(G_{\mu\nu}=8\pi G T_{\mu\nu}/c^4\): the left side
      describes curvature and the right side describes energy, momentum and stress. This equation is
      established physics. TTI&rsquo;s word “integration” is an interpretation of the relation, not a replacement
      for its mathematics.</p>
    <p>The popular rubber-sheet picture can help, but only up to a point. A heavy ball dimpling a sheet uses
      Earth&rsquo;s gravity to explain gravity and hides time, which is essential to relativity. A better plain
      description is that clocks and rulers behave differently around energy, so the straightest available
      route through spacetime can look curved when plotted in space. Light follows that geometry too, which
      is why gravitational lensing lets astronomers map mass that does not shine.</p>
    <p>“Dark matter” is the working name for the extra gravitating component inferred from several kinds of
      evidence: galaxy motions, lensing, galaxy clusters, the cosmic microwave background and the growth of
      structure. “Dark energy” names the component associated with accelerated cosmic expansion. The familiar
      percentages are parameters of a successful cosmological model fitted to data; they are not a direct
      inventory made by opening three boxes.</p>
    <p class="lede">Any TTI account must reproduce the same observations at least as well as the established
      model. Renaming dark matter “partial integration” does not reduce that burden by a single measurement.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">From interpretation to accountable model</p>
    <h2 id="t-gravity-bridge">Four Questions the Completion Picture Must Answer</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>What carries the missing gravity?</h3>
      <p>A completion fraction such as χ must be a defined field, distribution or state variable. Its units,
        dynamics and stress&ndash;energy contribution must be stated. A profile selected because it yields flat
        rotation curves is a hypothesis to derive, not an explanation by itself.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Why does light respond as observed?</h3>
      <p>Dark mass is mapped not only by orbit speeds but by lensing. The same χ distribution must bend light,
        move galaxies and shape clusters consistently. Separate adjustable profiles for each effect would make
        the proposal flexible rather than predictive.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>How did structure form?</h3>
      <p>Ordinary matter interacted strongly with radiation in the early universe. A successful dark component
        helped gravitational wells grow earlier. Partial integration must therefore supply an evolution law,
        not merely describe present-day halos.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>What observation would end the proposal?</h3>
      <p>A confirmed particle can weaken the structural reading; a conserved χ field that cannot fit lensing,
        clusters and expansion with one parameter set can defeat its physical implementation. A theory earns
        clarity by stating losses before fitting data.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">How to hold the 5 : 27 : 68 picture</p>
    <h2 id="t-budget-reading">A Map of Completion, Not a Derived Cosmic Budget</h2>
    <p>TTI groups baryonic matter, dark matter and dark energy as completed, partial and baseline integration.
      This is a conceptual ordering: ordinary matter couples to light and builds chemistry; dark matter
      gravitates without emitting light; dark energy remains smooth enough to be associated with large-scale
      expansion. The order offers a way to ask whether one process could underlie three observed behaviours.</p>
    <p>It does not derive the measured fractions. The numbers 5, 27 and 68 come from cosmological inference,
      with uncertainties and dependence on the model used in the fit. The framework currently reads those
      results after the fact. To predict them, it would need dynamics that evolve an initial state into those
      approximate abundances and continue to match observations at other redshifts.</p>
    <p>Nor does “incomplete” mean defective. It labels a proposed place in a transformation chain, not a moral
      rank. Dark matter would dominate gravitational structure in this account precisely because its partial
      state is abundant and persistent. Human observers belong to the small baryonic portion because chemistry
      requires electromagnetic interaction, not because observers are cosmically preferred.</p>
    <p class="muted">That separation preserves the page&rsquo;s useful intuition while preventing it from
      impersonating a finished physical theory: geometry is established, the dark-sector evidence is
      established, and the completion narrative remains a working hypothesis. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>
